WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
World Action Models jointly generate future video and robot actions.

2
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:11.000
Few-step sampling reduces computation. but coarse updates shift the states visited by the student.

3
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:15.000
APM supervises predictions from states reached by the student itself.

4
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:20.000
Stage I constructs inputs by interpolating training samples and noise.

5
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:25.000
The frozen teacher integrates its velocity field to provide finite-interval target states.

6
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:29.000
Stage II restarts the frozen teacher at a state reached by the student.

7
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:33.000
The first intermediate state provides the local target; the endpoint provides the anchor.

8
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:37.000
Modality-routed adapters learn both targets from the same starting state.

9
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:41.000
When gradients conflict. the endpoint gradient has priority.

10
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:48.000
We train on Clean demonstrations and evaluate directly on both Clean and Random settings.

11
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:53.000
At one step. APM improves Clean success by 2.0 points and Random success by 1.4 points over Continued.

12
00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:58.000
Its average success is 80.9%. The single-step Flash-WAM result is reported by DirectWAM.

13
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:03.000
APM improves average success over Continued at all three budgets. using one checkpoint.

14
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:08.000
At two steps. Flash-WAM has higher average success; APM reaches 91.4% at four steps.

15
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:12.667
Restart anchors improve Random success over Original anchors at one and two steps without local supervision.

16
00:01:12.667 --> 00:01:17.333
Adding local supervision alone does not consistently improve closed-loop success.

17
00:01:17.333 --> 00:01:22.000
With Restart and local supervision. projection improves average success over direct gradient summation.

18
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:25.333
Fixed-state tests separate local-transition error from endpoint error.

19
00:01:25.333 --> 00:01:28.667
Projection lowers video endpoint error versus unprojected supervision. while increasing local error.

20
00:01:28.667 --> 00:01:32.000
Its endpoint improvement over No Field has a 95% interval that crosses zero.

21
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:37.000
Compared with Continued. APM improves PSNR. SSIM. and LPIPS in single-step video prediction.

22
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:42.000
Against Flash-WAM. APM has higher PSNR; Flash-WAM has better SSIM and LPIPS under their deployment settings.

23
00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:47.000
Ground truth. LingBot-VA. Flash-WAM. and APM are compared at future frames 1. 4. and 8.

24
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:52.000
These cases were selected post hoc from 500 contexts where all three head-camera metrics favor APM over Flash-WAM.

25
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:56.000
Real-robot evaluation uses matched initial states and 24 trials per method on each task.

26
00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:04.000
Both robots approach and grasp the pen.

27
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:12.000
Both methods align the pen with the holder.

28
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:20.000
In these selected trials. the coarse teacher fails and APM completes the insertion.

29
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:26.667
Both methods grasp and lift the towel.

30
00:02:26.667 --> 00:02:33.333
After release. the teacher leaves an incomplete fold; APM completes the task.

31
00:02:33.333 --> 00:02:40.000
These are selected qualitative trials. not aggregate success rates.

32
00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:45.000
Single-step APM improves success over the one-step teacher on both real-robot tasks.

33
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:50.000
Compared with the eight-step teacher. APM is 21.3 times faster. with lower PenHolder and higher FoldTowel success.

34
00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:56.000
Paired teacher targets at student-reached states support few-step joint video–action generation.
